//@version=6
// MAR1 Scanner - which pairs have a series open right now.
//
// The question this answers is the one a chart cannot: maRejection.pine tells you
// everything about the pair in front of you and nothing about the other twenty-nine.
// This runs the same state machine on a list of symbols at once and prints only
// the pairs where a window is currently OPEN - a gate that opened and has not yet
// been closed by a win.
//
// What "open" means here
// ----------------------
// Exactly what it means in maRejection.pine, because it is the same code path:
//
//   ARMED    the gate opened - a failure marker landed on the streak threshold,
//            so the next signal on that pair is a live entry. No entry yet.
//   RUNNING  N entries have been taken since the window opened and none of them
//            has come out right yet. This is the "series not closed" state.
//   idle     nothing pending. Not shown unless you ask for it.
//
// A window opens on a green diamond, or on the first entry when the streak gate
// is Off and there are no green diamonds to open it. It closes on a win, and only
// on a win. Nothing else - not time, not a change of trend - closes it.
//
// And the other half: what got finished
// -------------------------------------
// The second table is the log of windows that CLOSED, one row each: pair, the
// minute the winning candle ended, how many entries it cost, and which way it
// went. It starts over each day by default.
//
// The two tables are the same fact from opposite ends, and they are worth
// reading as a pair. The scanner says where your attention is owed right now;
// the log says what the day actually cost - and its average, entries per closed
// window, is the number the streak gate exists to bring down.
//
// The cost, stated plainly
// ------------------------
// TradingView computes an indicator only for the symbol on the chart. The only
// way to learn anything about the other pairs is request.security(), which
// re-runs the whole calculation on each requested symbol's data. So this script
// is roughly thirty times the work of the original, and that lands inside one
// execution budget.
//
// Three levers when it times out, in the order worth trying:
//
//   1. blank out slots you do not need. A blank slot still spends one of the 40
//      security calls TradingView allows, but on the chart's own symbol, whose
//      data is already loaded.
//   2. run it on a higher timeframe. Fewer bars, same logic.
//   3. 'Bars of history per pair' - last, and reluctantly. It is the only lever
//      that changes the ANSWER rather than the cost: a shorter history gives the
//      streak gate a different threshold from the chart's, and the table stops
//      agreeing with the large diamonds. See the input for the full reasoning.
//
// Two things it does NOT reproduce
// --------------------------------
// The scanner carries its own copy of every signal input, because Pine cannot
// read another script's settings. KEEP THEM IN SYNC WITH maRejection.pine or the
// two will disagree about what is open, and the scanner will be the one that is
// wrong - the chart is the source of truth. The defaults below are the defaults
// there, so an untouched pair of scripts already agrees.
//
// And the data feed matters: OANDA:EURUSD and FX_IDC:EURUSD are different price
// series and will occasionally grade a candle differently. Point the slots at the
// same exchange your charts use.
indicator('MAR1 Scanner', overlay = true, max_bars_back = 200)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Gate and window - the three settings the log below answers to
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// First in the dialog, and first for one reason: these are the settings that get
// changed while the finished-series log is open, and that log's entries-per-win
// average is the number they move. Reading the average and reaching the setting
// that changes it now happen on one screen, without a scroll between them.
//
// Pine has no way to order the settings dialog - sections appear in the order
// their inputs are DECLARED - so first in the dialog and first in the file are
// the same fact, and this is what being first costs: every setting below shifts
// by three positions, and TradingView maps saved settings onto inputs BY
// POSITION. See the note on the log section for the recovery.
//
//   Percentage            the cumulative cutoff the streak distribution is read
//                         at, which becomes the gate threshold in Distribution
//                         and in Max of both
//   Min entries to start  the manual floor under that threshold
//   A window is closed by which successes close a window - the definition every
//                         number in the log is counted against
//
// All three are folded into CFGSUM, so maRejection.pine disagreeing about any of
// them is caught rather than discovered. They are still Filters settings in
// every sense that matters to the signal; only the header above them changed.
// NOTHING BELOW PRINTS ITSELF IN THE STATUS LINE, and that is what
// display = display.none on nearly every input in this file buys.
//
// TradingView prints an indicator's ARGUMENTS after its name in the legend, in
// declaration order. This file has a hundred and two inputs - thirty pair slots
// with their tick boxes are sixty of them on their own - and the line ran the
// width of the chart with nothing saying which number was which. A legend that
// has to be decoded is worse than no legend.
//
// display.none removes an input from that line and from the Data Window and
// changes NOTHING ELSE: the input is fully live, fully editable, and sits in the
// settings dialog exactly where it did. It is a display flag, not a switch, and
// it does not touch the saved-settings POSITION that everything else in this
// file has to be careful about.
//
// THREE ARE DELIBERATELY LEFT VISIBLE, the same three maRejection.pine keeps -
// which is the point, since the two are meant to be read against each other:
//
//   MA Length      5 against 27 is not the same scanner twice. It is the one
//                  setting every column in every table is measured with.
//   Streak gate    with Off there is no gate at all, nothing is held back, and
//                  every row is a live entry. Reading the tables without knowing
//                  this is reading fiction.
//   Fire signals   whether the hours filter is actually biting. A quiet table
//   only inside    means 'nothing triggered' when this is off and 'nothing was
//   those hours    allowed to' when it is on, and those are not the same fact.
//
// Everything else that a screenshot would have to carry is already folded into
// CFGSUM at the foot, which catches a divergence from maRejection.pine that no
// amount of squinting at a legend would.
//
// TO BRING ANY OTHER ONE BACK: delete ', display = display.none' from its line.
// To hide one of the three above: add it. There is nothing to keep in sync - the
// flag is per input and reaches no logic.
gTune = 'Gate and window (must match MAR1)'

basePercentage    = input.float(95.0, 'Percentage', minval = 1.0, maxval = 100.0, step = 0.05, group = gTune, display = display.none)
minEntriesToStart = input.int(5, 'Min entries to start', minval = 1, group = gTune, display = display.none)

// Which successes close a window. 'Entries only' is the strict reading and the
// default on both sides: a window is closed by a trade that was actually taken,
// not by a signal the gate held back.
successScope = input.string('Entries only', 'A window is closed by',
     options = ['Entries only', 'Entries and warnings', 'All found signals'], group = gTune, display = display.none)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The log of finished series
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The scanner table further down answers "what is open". This answers the
// opposite question - what got finished today - and it is the one that
// accumulates into something worth reading back: pair, the minute the winning
// candle closed, and how many entries that window cost.
//
// It is built from history rather than watched live, so it is complete the
// moment the script loads: the state machine reports every closure it passes,
// and the log keeps whichever ones fall inside the current period.
//
// Why this sits at the top of the file
// ------------------------------------
// Pine has no way to order the settings dialog: the sections appear in the order
// their inputs are DECLARED, so being second in the dialog and being second in
// the file are the same fact. This section and the custom window below it are
// the two that get opened while trading rather than while setting the script up,
// and they used to sit fourth and fifth, behind the whole Scanner group.
//
// Nothing is in front of them now except the three gate settings above, and that
// pairing is the point rather than a side effect: those three and this log fit on
// ONE screen of the dialog together, so the number being read and the settings
// that move it are never a scroll apart. Signal, Filters and everything else are
// set up once and then left alone, which is why they are all now below.
//
// The cost is the one this file has already paid once, at 'The custom log
// window' below: TradingView maps saved settings onto inputs BY POSITION, so
// moving these fifteen widgets in front of Signal, Filters, Scanner and the
// thirty pair slots slides every one of those onto a value that was saved
// against something else. The recovery is Defaults -> Reset settings, once, on
// each chart already carrying the script - and it works because the defaults in
// this file ARE the working setup, the twenty-one pairs below included.
//
// Anything ADDED later still goes to the bottom, next to the skip list, for
// exactly the reason this move had to be paid for.
gJrn = 'Finished series log'

// How much of the log to put on the chart. The log itself is kept either way -
// this only decides what is drawn.
//
// Full list by DEFAULT, which it was not until the log learned to wrap.
//
// Hidden was the honest default while a day of closures across twenty-one pairs
// was one column taller than any chart window - the list arrived covering the
// price it was supposed to be read against, and the two channels that cost no
// screen space, the Pine Logs panel and the CSV export, were both already on.
//
// Blocks are what changed that: fifty-nine closures at twenty rows a block is
// three short columns, which sits beside the price rather than over it. And this
// file's defaults now have to stand on their own - moving this whole section to
// the top of the dialog means a reset is how settings come back, and a reset
// that hides the log looks exactly like a log that broke.
//
// Summary only is the middle setting: a single line with the count and the
// average per window, which is the number the streak gate exists to bring down.
journalShow = input.string('Full list', 'Show the log as',
     options = ['Full list', 'Summary only', 'Hidden'], group = gJrn, display = display.none)

// The way out of TradingView.
//
// Pine cannot write a file - it has no access to the disk, the network or the
// clipboard. What TradingView itself has is 'Export chart data', which writes
// every PLOT of every indicator on the chart into a CSV, one row per candle with
// the candle's time in the first column. So the log leaves as thirty plots, one
// per slot, each carrying nothing but the closures of its own pair.
//
// Set to data_window: no line on the chart, no value in the status line, but a
// real series that the exporter can see. The chart stays exactly as clean as it
// is with the table hidden.
exportClosures = input.bool(true, 'Publish closures for CSV export', group = gJrn,
     tooltip = 'Right-click the chart, Export chart data, pick the range, Export. The CLOSED 01..30 columns are empty except on the candle where that slot closed a series - the value is the entry count, positive for a long and negative for a short.', display = display.none)

// The window the log covers, split into the two questions it actually has:
// HOW LONG a stretch, and WHERE that stretch sits.
//
// It used to be one dropdown with a 'Custom window' entry in it, which asked you
// to set a mode and then two numbers that were ignored in every other mode - so
// nothing appeared to happen until all three agreed. Splitting it removes that:
// the length is always live, and the anchor is the only thing that decides
// whether the stretch follows the clock or stands still.
//
//   length N hours         with 'Ending now'        the last N hours, rolling
//                          with 'Starting at hour'  a fixed slice of today
//   length Day / Week      the calendar unit, from midnight UTC+1 or Monday.
//                          The anchor does not apply - these carry their own
//                          boundaries - and the start hour is ignored.
//   length Everything      no window at all, until the row cap evicts the oldest
//
// Rolling is the working setting: a day is the smallest calendar unit there is,
// and by the afternoon a day of closures is longer than the screen.
//
// Fixed is the reading-back setting. A rolling window cannot show you the London
// open once it is three hours behind you, and a day shows it buried under
// everything since. Naming the hour is what makes a slice of the morning
// readable on its own while the afternoon is still going.
// Custom takes its length from 'Custom window length' at the bottom of this
// group, and it is there rather than here for a reason given in full at that
// input: TradingView maps saved settings onto inputs by POSITION, so a new one
// declared in the middle of this file would shift every setting after it - all
// thirty pair slots included.
//
// The steps run past a session and up to a full day because a rolling window is
// the only thing that can span midnight: 24 hours is the last day of trading,
// where Day is the last day of the CALENDAR, and at nine in the morning those
// are not remotely the same list. 12 and 16 cover the same distance for a desk
// that starts before the London open.
journalLength = input.string('Day', 'Window length',
     options = ['1 hour', '2 hours', '4 hours', '8 hours', '12 hours', '16 hours', '24 hours', 'Custom', 'Day', 'Week', 'Everything'], group = gJrn,
     tooltip = 'Day and Week ignore the anchor below - they start at midnight UTC+1 and at Monday. 24 hours is not the same as Day: it rolls back a full day from now, across midnight. Custom reads the number of hours from the input at the bottom of this group.', display = display.none)

journalAnchor = input.string('Ending now', 'Window anchored',
     options = ['Ending now', 'Starting at hour', 'Starting at date'], group = gJrn,
     tooltip = 'Ending now rolls with the clock. Starting at hour pins a stretch of TODAY. Starting at date pins a stretch of a named day, which is the only one that can read back a day that has already ended - it takes its start from the date picker at the bottom of this group.', display = display.none)

// UTC+1, the frame every other timestamp in this project is written in. The
// window stays inside today: 22:00 for four hours simply ends when today does.
journalFromHour = input.int(9, 'Starting hour, UTC+1', minval = 0, maxval = 23, group = gJrn,
     tooltip = 'Only used when the anchor above is Starting at hour and the length is in hours.', display = display.none)

// How many closures the log KEEPS, which since the cap went past a hundred is no
// longer the same question as how many it draws.
//
// ZERO is the default, and it means keep every closure the window holds. That is
// what this field should have shipped with, because a number here is a promise
// about how much of the period the summary line describes, and any number
// smaller than the period itself breaks that promise silently. At the old cap of
// 40 the entries-per-win average covered a fifth of an M1 day while the header
// directly above it named the whole day - the one number the streak gate exists
// to move, read off the wrong sample and labelled as though it were not. A
// hundred moved that line without removing it; zero removes it.
//
// The table is unaffected either way. It stops at a block height times six
// blocks whatever this says, which is six hundred rows at the tallest a block
// can be - past that a table is taller and wider than any screen, and Pine's own
// row limit is in that neighbourhood too. So this setting is about the SUMMARY,
// the CSV export and the Pine Logs panel, never about what is on screen.
//
// Typing a number is still worth it in one case, and it is not a correctness
// one: the loops below are bounded by the length of the list, so a deliberately
// short log is the cheap way to keep this script inside its execution budget on
// a slow machine or a long history. Reach for it when the script times out, not
// otherwise.
//
// What actually decides how far back the log reaches is 'Window length' above,
// not this. With Day - the default there - midnight empties the list whatever
// this says. Zero here AND Everything there is the whole loaded history.
journalCap = input.int(0, 'Keep at most N closures (0 = all)', minval = 0, maxval = 50000, group = gJrn,
     tooltip = "Zero keeps every closure inside the window, which is what makes the average on the header line describe the period the header names. Type a number only to cut the script's work - the table draws what the block layout holds either way. How far back the log reaches is decided by 'Window length', not by this.", display = display.none)

// The ceiling that applies when the cap is 0, and it exists for exactly one
// reason: Pine refuses an array past 100,000 elements and there are six of them
// here. Fifty thousand closures is more than thirty pairs produce in a month of
// M1, so nothing real reaches it - but "keep everything" still has to stop
// somewhere, and stopping at a number beats stopping at a runtime error.
//
// When it does bite, it says so on the header line rather than quietly dropping
// rows, which is the whole difference between a ceiling and a bug.
JRN_KEEP_MAX = 50000

journalKeep = journalCap <= 0 ? JRN_KEEP_MAX : journalCap

// The tallest ONE column of the log can be. Not an input: it is a property of
// tables rather than a preference, and jrnTable is declared for exactly this
// many rows plus the two headers.
//
// It stopped being the ceiling on the whole list when the list learned to wrap:
// blocks stand beside each other, so six of them hold six times this and cost no
// height at all. What it is now is the tallest a block may be asked to be - by
// the setting, or by auto once all six blocks are already open - which puts the
// ceiling on the whole log at six hundred.
JRN_DRAW_MAX = 100

// How much of the date the Opened and Closed columns carry.
//
// The time alone was written for M1, where a log is one afternoon and every row
// in it is obviously today. It stops working the moment a row is NOT obviously
// today: on H1 a window runs for hours and a forty-row log covers most of a
// week, on H4 it covers a month, and '13:49' then names a moment that could be
// any of six days. Two rows an hour apart on the clock and four days apart in
// fact look identical, which is the one thing a log must never do.
//
// Auto is the default and it prints the day and the month on every row of both
// tables. It used to work the span out from the rows themselves and print only
// what they needed - bare times on a single day, the day number once the log
// crossed midnight, the month once it crossed a month.
//
// That was right about the list and wrong about the reader. It made the date
// appear and disappear on the same screen as the log rolled, which is precisely
// what a column kept for comparing one day against another cannot do. A stamp
// you cannot rely on being there is one you cannot write anything down against.
//
// The year is the one part still left out, and it is the part most likely to
// need adding back now that the cap defaults to keeping everything: the column
// has to fit beside four others, twice over in the two-block layout, and a log
// that reaches across a new year would date two different Januaries the same
// way. 'yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm' is in the list for exactly that.
journalStamp = input.string('Auto', 'Timestamp format', group = gJrn,
     options = ['Auto', 'HH:mm', 'dd HH:mm', 'MM-dd HH:mm', 'yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm'],
     tooltip = 'Auto prints day and month on every row - MM-dd HH:mm - in both tables. Pick a fixed format to override it.', display = display.none)

// The second way out, and the one that does not depend on the plan. Pine Logs
// is a panel in the editor, not on the chart: it costs no screen space at all,
// and its text can be selected and copied into a file by hand.
//
// It exists because 'Export chart data' is a paid feature. When it is not there,
// this is the whole of what is left - Pine cannot reach the disk, the network or
// the clipboard by itself.
//
// One line per closed series, semicolon separated so a paste into a spreadsheet
// splits into columns in one step:
//
//   MAR1CLOSE;EURUSD;2026-07-27 14:36;3;long
//
// Scoped to the same window the table is - see periodFrom below for why that can
// be decided while the script is still walking through history.
logClosures = input.bool(true, 'Write closures to Pine Logs', group = gJrn,
     tooltip = 'Open the Pine Editor and switch to the Pine Logs tab. Select the lines and copy them. This is the export that works without a paid plan.', display = display.none)

journalPos = input.string('Middle left', 'Log position',
     options = ['Middle left', 'Bottom center', 'Middle center', 'Top center', 'Top right', 'Middle right', 'Bottom right'], group = gJrn, display = display.none)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The custom log window
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// These feed 'Window length' and 'Window anchored' directly above, and they are
// declared here, next to what they control, rather than at the bottom of the
// dialog where they used to sit.
//
// That position was not a preference and moving them was not free. TradingView
// maps saved settings onto inputs BY POSITION, so putting four inputs in front
// of the thirty pair slots slides every one of those slots - and the skip list
// with them - onto values that were saved against something else. Which is why
// they were parked at the end in the first place.
//
// It was done anyway, deliberately, because the cost is a one-off and the
// annoyance was permanent: three settings that only make sense while reading the
// log lived four hundred lines and sixty widgets away from it, under a header of
// their own, and had to be hunted for every time. The section above has since
// been moved for the same reason and at the same price, so the two now travel
// together at the front of the file.
//
// What pays for it is that the defaults in this file ARE the working setup - the
// twenty-one pairs below are the real watchlist, in order. So the recovery is
// Defaults -> Reset settings, once, and the list comes back rather than having to
// be retyped. Whatever is added later still goes to the BOTTOM, next to the skip
// list, for exactly the reason this move had to be paid for.
//
// The section is what the fixed 1/2/4/8 dropdown could not express, and the two
// gaps were different:
//
//   the LENGTH was quantised   there was no way to ask for three hours, or for
//                              forty minutes, and no combination of the fixed
//                              steps adds up to either
//   the START was always today every anchor was built from todayStart or from
//                              the wall clock, so a day that had already ended
//                              was unreachable - the log could describe this
//                              morning and never last Monday
//
// The length is a float because a typed length is not a whole number of hours:
// 2.5 and 0.75 are ordinary things to want, and rounding them to the hour would
// answer a question nobody asked. Capped at a week, which is where the row cap
// rather than the window becomes what limits the answer.
gWin = 'Custom log window'

customHours = input.float(3.0, 'Custom window length, hours', minval = 0.05, maxval = 168.0, step = 0.25, group = gWin,
     tooltip = "Used only when 'Window length' is set to Custom. Fractions are honoured - 2.5 is two and a half hours, 0.75 is forty-five minutes.", display = display.none)

// input.time gives a date AND a time from one picker, which is why it is one
// input rather than a date and an hour that have to be kept consistent by hand.
//
// One thing to know about it, said here rather than discovered later: the value
// the picker returns is read as a plain timestamp, and the picker itself works
// in the chart's exchange time, while every other stamp in this project is
// UTC+1. Rather than convert behind your back and hope, the log's header line
// prints the window it actually ended up with, in UTC+1 - so what you selected
// is checkable on screen instead of assumed.
// The default is a plain number, and it is a number rather than a timestamp()
// call because input.time demands a CONST int and timestamp() does not give one.
//
// Both of its forms were tried. timestamp(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0) comes back as SIMPLE
// int - the numeric form reads the exchange's timezone, and a value that depends
// on which symbol the chart is on cannot be a compile-time constant. The string
// form is the documented way to get a const, but this input has already cost two
// paste cycles, and a literal cannot be wrong about its own type at all.
//
// 1767225600000 is 2026-01-01 00:00 UTC in milliseconds. To move it: seconds
// since the epoch times a thousand. It is only where the date picker opens, so
// the exact value carries no meaning beyond being a neutral starting point.
customFrom = input.time(1767225600000, 'Custom window starts', group = gWin,
     tooltip = "Used only when 'Window anchored' is set to Starting at date. The window runs from here for the length above. Check the log's header line - it prints the window that was actually applied, in UTC+1.", display = display.none)

// The Today button the picker does not have.
//
// TradingView's calendar is its own widget and nothing in Pine can put a button
// inside it - it underlines today's number and that is the whole of the help it
// gives. Reading back last Monday takes two clicks; coming back to today takes
// finding the month, finding the number, and then reading the header to check.
//
// A tick box beside the picker is the nearest thing that can exist, and it is
// better than a button in one way: a button would set the date and be forgotten,
// so tomorrow the log would still be showing today's date, pinned. This follows
// the calendar instead - ticked, the window starts at midnight of whatever day
// it currently is.
//
// The picked date is left untouched underneath, so unticking returns to it
// rather than to a date that got overwritten.
customToday = input.bool(false, 'Custom window starts today (ignores the date)', group = gWin,
     tooltip = "Overrides the date above with today's midnight, and follows the calendar - it still means today tomorrow. The date you picked is kept and comes back when this is unticked.", display = display.none)

// The date default, repeated as a constant so the header can tell a date that
// was CHOSEN from one that was never touched.
//
// Without that distinction the only honest warning would be permanent - the
// date is inert under every anchor except one, so a fixed note would sit on the
// header for everyone who has never opened the picker, and a warning that is
// always there is one nobody reads.
CUSTOM_FROM_DEFAULT = 1767225600000

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The closed log, in two columns
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A hundred rows is taller than any screen, which is what the row cap was
// supposed to solve and does not: twenty rows already run off a chart window,
// and the cap only decides where the list stops, not how it is shaped.
//
// So the list wraps into another block beside it. Same five columns repeated, a
// blank column between them, and each block picks up where the one before it ran
// out - which divides the height by the number of blocks for the same rows.
//
// It wraps as many times as it needs to, up to six blocks. The first version
// stopped at two, and that turned out to be a ceiling rather than a layout: with
// twenty rows a column it listed forty of fifty-nine closures and said so in the
// header, which is the log going from too tall to too short in one step. Rows
// per column is now a shape, not a limit.
//
// Zero is AUTO and does the shaping itself: columns stay twenty tall while there
// are blocks left to open, and only once all six are full does the height grow,
// to a hundred. That holds six hundred closures side by side instead of the old
// hundred stacked.
//
// It used to mean the single tall column, and that was the wrong meaning for a
// zero to carry here. 'Keep at most N closures', a few settings above, reads
// zero as "no limit" - so the same key turned the opposite way in two
// neighbouring fields, and the one that produced the WORST layout was the one
// that looked like "no limit". Typing 100 gets the tall column back.
journalPerCol = input.int(20, 'Closed log: rows per column (0 = auto)', minval = 0, maxval = 100, group = gWin,
     tooltip = 'How tall each block of the finished-series list is. The list wraps into as many blocks side by side as it needs, up to six - so 20 here holds 120 closures. 0 is auto: twenty tall until all six blocks are in use, then taller, up to 600 rows in all. Type 100 for one tall column.', display = display.none)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Signal settings - mirrors of maRejection.pine
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
gSig  = 'Signal (must match MAR1)'
gFilt = 'Filters (must match MAR1)'

maLength = input.int(5, 'MA Length', minval = 2, group = gSig)
maType   = input.string('EMA', 'MA Type', options = ['SMA', 'EMA', 'WMA', 'RMA'], group = gSig, display = display.none)

// A string rather than input.source, and the difference is not cosmetic. An
// input.source is bound to the series of the CHART's symbol, so passing one into
// request.security would feed every pair the price of whatever chart this script
// happens to sit on. Naming the source and resolving it inside the requested
// context is the only way each pair gets its own prices.
srcName = input.string('ohlc4', 'Source', options = ['close', 'open', 'high', 'low', 'hl2', 'hlc3', 'ohlc4'], group = gSig, display = display.none)

trendBars = input.int(3, 'Bars that must close on the trend side of MA', minval = 2, maxval = 10, group = gSig, display = display.none)
trendSep  = input.float(0.30, 'Min distance of trend closes from MA (x ATR)', minval = 0.0, maxval = 2.0, step = 0.05, group = gSig, display = display.none)
wickRatio = input.float(0.60, 'Min wick share of candle range', minval = 0.30, maxval = 0.95, step = 0.05, group = gSig, display = display.none)
touchTol  = input.float(0.30, 'Almost-reached tolerance (x ATR)', minval = 0.0, maxval = 2.0, step = 0.05, group = gSig, display = display.none)
atrLen    = input.int(3, 'ATR length', minval = 2, group = gSig, display = display.none)

allowBodyInside    = input.bool(true, 'Accept small body fully on the trend side', group = gSig, display = display.none)
allowLongWick      = input.bool(true, 'Accept wick longer than its running average', group = gSig, display = display.none)
allowCounterCandle = input.bool(true, 'Accept a single counter-trend candle', group = gSig, display = display.none)

// --- MODULE: two counter-trend candles -------------------------------------
//
// The switches for the block of the same name inside mar1State(). They are
// constants rather than inputs so that adding them shifts nothing: TradingView
// maps saved settings onto inputs by position, and MAR1 has to declare its copy
// above the trigger, mid-file, where an input would displace every setting
// under it on every chart already carrying the script.
//
// The price of that is that nothing checks them: CFGSUM covers inputs only, so
// if these three ever stop matching maRejection.pine's, the scanner and the
// chart will quietly be running different entry rules and the banner will still
// say they agree. Change them in both files or in neither.
//
// What the module does, and why, is written out in maRejection.pine.
DC_ON   = true      // false rolls the module back without deleting it
DC_BACK = 4         // candles back from the second counter candle
DC_REF  = 'Close'   // 'Close' or 'Open' - which price of that candle bounds it
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// --- MODULE: flat filter ----------------------------------------------------
// Stand aside while price is walking a long way and arriving nowhere. Measured
// as an efficiency ratio - net displacement over the path actually walked, over
// flatLen bars - with two thresholds so the verdict cannot flicker, and a tail
// of flatWait bars after it clears so the first breakout candle is skipped.
//
// It suppresses the DETECTED signal, exactly where the hours filter does, so an
// open window keeps its entries and its rung and simply stands still until the
// flat ends: the ladder is DEFERRED, never abandoned, and the streak does not
// move while it is paused.
//
// The state machine lives inside mar1State, in the shared block, because the
// verdict belongs to the PAIR - it reads no moving average at all.
//
// THE FIVE INPUTS ARE DECLARED DOWN BY THE PAIR LIST, after sym30 and before
// the request.security calls - not here. Thirty pair slots, sixty inputs, sit
// between here and mar1State()'s own definition line, and TradingView maps a
// saved setting onto an input by POSITION: declared here, these five would
// shift every one of the sixty pair inputs on any chart already carrying this
// script.
//
// Placing them past the pair list works because mar1State() takes them as
// PARAMETERS rather than reading them as free globals - see its signature
// below. A parameter only has to be in scope at the CALL SITE, not at the
// function's own definition line, so the inputs can sit anywhere before the
// thirty request.security calls without mar1State() itself needing to move.
//
// flatOn = false restores the previous behaviour exactly.
//
// What the module does, and why it is an efficiency ratio rather than any width
// test, is written out in full in maRejection.pine. These five must stay
// identical there, here, in mar1Gates.pine, mar1GatesScanner.pine,
// maSweep.pine and maSweepPairs.pine. Nothing checks that for you.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

refLen = input.int(200, 'Average window (candles)', minval = 10, maxval = 200, group = gSig, display = display.none)

minPips              = input.float(1.0, 'Min wick length (pips)', minval = 0.0, step = 0.1, group = gFilt, display = display.none)
cooldown             = input.int(1, 'Min bars between signals', minval = 1, group = gFilt, display = display.none)
perDirectionCooldown = input.bool(true, 'Cooldown per direction', group = gFilt, display = display.none)
gateMode             = input.string('Max of both', 'Streak gate', options = ['Off', 'Manual', 'Distribution', 'Max of both'], group = gFilt)

// On by default, and the default must stay identical to maRejection.pine's. The
// hours filter runs before the streak is counted, so a mismatch here feeds the
// two scripts different distributions and hands them different gate thresholds.
startHour       = input.int(4, "Start indicator's hours", group = gFilt, display = display.none)
endHour         = input.int(16, "End indicator's hours", group = gFilt, display = display.none)
restrictToHours = input.bool(true, 'Fire signals only inside those hours', group = gFilt)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Scanner settings
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
gScan = 'Scanner'

// The performance lever, and 0 - no limit - is the default for a reason worth
// stating, because the old default of 5000 was quietly the largest source of
// disagreement between this script and the chart.
//
// maRejection.pine builds its streak distribution from the ENTIRE loaded history:
// its statistics block carries no window at all. A window here meant a different
// sample, a different percentile, and therefore a different gate threshold - and
// the gate is what decides whether a signal becomes an ENTRY or merely a warning.
// So a shorter history did not just blur a statistic, it changed which candles
// the scanner called entries, and the table stopped matching the large diamonds
// on the chart.
//
// What the window actually saved was close to nothing. Every expensive thing in
// mar1State - both refLen averages, the ATR, the trendBars loop - is computed
// outside it on every bar regardless. All the window ever skipped was O(1)
// counter bookkeeping and a cached sixteen-element loop.
//
// Set a number here only if the script genuinely times out, and expect the gate
// to drift from the chart's when you do.
scanBars = input.int(0, 'Bars of history per pair (0 = all)', minval = 0, maxval = 20000, group = gScan,
     tooltip = 'Leave at 0 so the streak gate is built from the same history the chart uses. Set a limit only if the script times out - a limited history gives a different gate threshold, and the table will stop agreeing with the diamonds.', display = display.none)

showIdle = input.bool(false, 'Also list pairs with nothing open', group = gScan,
     tooltip = 'Off by default - the whole point is a short list you can read at a glance.', display = display.none)

// Bottom right by default. The top right corner is where TradingView stacks its
// own chart controls and where MAR1's 'Fails After Success' table lands, so a
// scanner anchored there is read over the top of something else - which is worse
// than not being there at all, because a half-covered table still looks answered.
//
// The one thing to keep clear of down here is MAR1's TAKEN table, which owns
// bottom right on its own chart. Two tables on one anchor overlap, so if both
// scripts sit on the same chart, move one of them.
tablePos = input.string('Bottom right', 'Table position',
     options = ['Bottom right', 'Bottom center', 'Middle right', 'Middle left', 'Middle center', 'Top right', 'Top center'], group = gScan, display = display.none)

// Small rather than Pine's Normal. Thirty rows plus the closed log is a lot of
// table for a corner of a chart, and this is the one setting that decides
// whether all of it fits without covering the price it is supposed to be read
// against. Normal is still one step away in the list if a screen can take it.
scanTextSize = input.string('Small', 'Table text size', options = ['Tiny', 'Small', 'Normal', 'Large', 'Huge'], group = gScan, display = display.none)

// The slots. Thirty of the 40 request calls TradingView allows, which leaves the
// call count comfortable and puts the whole of the remaining headroom into
// execution time - the calculation behind each slot is the whole of MAR1, not a
// moving average, so time is the budget that runs out first.
//
// If the script starts timing out, the fix is 'Bars of history per pair' rather
// than fewer pairs: halving the history halves the work on all thirty at once,
// while blanking a slot saves one thirtieth and loses a pair.
//
// Blank means "skip this slot". It still costs a call - see the header - but it
// is pointed at the chart's own symbol, which is already loaded, and no row is
// printed for it.
//
// Why every slot carries a tick box
// ---------------------------------
// The symbol picker is what you want for choosing a pair, because choosing a
// pair means choosing a data provider as much as a ticker - OANDA:EURUSD and
// FX_IDC:EURUSD are different price series, and only the picker shows you which
// providers exist for a symbol and lets you pick between them. A plain text
// field cannot do that: it can only take a prefix you already know by heart.
//
// What the picker cannot do is give a slot back. It has no empty state - it will
// only exchange one symbol for another - so a slot filled in by mistake stays
// filled forever, and a list of thirty is a list that will be mistyped at least
// once.
//
// The tick box is that missing state, kept beside the picker rather than
// replacing it. Unticked, the slot is skipped: no row, not counted, and the
// symbol underneath is left alone so ticking it again brings the pair back
// without retyping. Both widgets share an inline id, so they occupy one row of
// the dialog between them.
//
// An unticked slot still spends a request call - see the header - because Pine
// cannot make that call conditional. It costs time, not correctness.
gList1 = 'Pairs 1-10'
gList2 = 'Pairs 11-20'
gList3 = 'Pairs 21-30'

// The defaults are the watchlist this was built for, on the FX feed and in the
// order it is kept in: the USD majors first, then the EUR crosses, the GBP
// crosses, and the remaining CHF, CAD and AUD legs. Twenty-one pairs; slots 22
// to 30 are left empty for whatever gets added later.
// display = display.none on every slot, and it is the fix for the legend rather
// than a detail. TradingView prints an indicator's arguments after its name in
// the status line, and sixty of them - thirty tick boxes and thirty tickers -
// is a line of text across the whole top of the chart. display.none keeps a slot
// out of that line while leaving it fully live in the settings dialog.
//
// The count is not lost: the footer of the scanner table says how many pairs
// were actually scanned, which is the number worth seeing anyway.
use01 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 1',  inline = 'p01', group = gList1, display = display.none,
     tooltip = 'The tick is the off switch. The symbol picker has no empty state - it will only swap one symbol for another - so this is what takes a slot out of the scan, including one filled in by mistake.')
sym01 = input.symbol('FX:EURUSD', '', inline = 'p01', group = gList1, display = display.none)
use02 = input.bool(true,  'Pair 2',  inline = 'p02', group = gList1, display = display.none)
sym02 = input.symbol('FX:GBPUSD', '', inline = 'p02', group = gList1, display = display.none)
